  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "Write $3+\\frac{2}{a}$ as a single fraction.",
  "worked_solution": "Using the fact that $3=\\frac{3}{1},$ we can write the expression as\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a}.\n$$\n\n \n\nIn order to add the two fractions, we need them to have a common denominator. So, we put both fractions over a common denominator of $a$ by multiplying the top and bottom of the first fraction by $a:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{a}{a}\\cdot \\frac{3}{1}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a}{a}+\\frac{2}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we add the fractions and simplify the result:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3a}{a}+\\frac{2}{a} & = \\\\ \\frac{3a+2}{a}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
